  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "When a line is perpendicular to a plane, must the line's direction vector also be a unit vector?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "It must",
    "B": "Its magnitude must equal 2",
    "C": "It must be the zero vector",
    "D": "It need not"
  },
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Method 1: Separate direction from magnitude\n\nCollinearity between the line's nonzero direction vector and a nonzero normal vector to the plane is sufficient for perpendicularity; the length of the direction vector need not be \\(1\\). Thus the answer is D.\n\nMethod 2: Construct a nonunit direction vector\n\nChoose points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) on the given line with \\(AB=2\\). Then \\(d=\\overrightarrow{AB}\\) is a nonzero direction vector of this same perpendicular line, but its magnitude is \\(|d|=2\\ne1\\). Thus a direction vector need not be a unit vector, so the answer is D.",
